        "original_id": "62f61780-57c9-5e5c-a1d3-636625cc5a4a",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Places: Loch Shiel, River Shiel, Loch Moidart, Castle Tioram, Eilean Tioram, Dalelia, Dalilea, Acharacle, Fort William, Lochaber, Highland, Scotland\n- Concepts: Scottish Gaelic place names, physical geography\n\nFacts:\n- Loch Shiel changes considerably along its length, being deep and enclosed by mountains in the northeast and shallow with bog and rough pasture in the southwest.\n- The 4 km River Shiel drains from the southwest end of Loch Shiel to the sea in Loch Moidart near Castle Tioram.\n- Castle Tioram is a ruined castle on the tidal island Eilean Tioram in Loch Moidart, Lochaber, Highland, Scotland.\n- Castle Tioram has the Scottish Gaelic name \"Caisteal Tioram,\" meaning \"dry castle.\"\n- Castle Tioram is west of Acharacle and about 80 km from Fort William.\n- Dalelia or Dalilea is a collection of houses on the north shore of Loch Shiel in the Acharacle district of Argyll in the Scottish Highlands.\n- Dalelia derives from the Scottish Gaelic name \"Dàil Eileadh.\"\n- Dalelia is in the Scottish council area of Highland.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
